WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.010
(offbeat uplifting music)

00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:04.731
- [Narrator] Schools
used to look like this,

00:00:04.731 --> 00:00:06.724
classrooms packed with students,

00:00:06.724 --> 00:00:09.629
lunchrooms full of loud conversations,

00:00:09.629 --> 00:00:12.483
and teachers just trying to
catch a break in the lounge.

00:00:13.949 --> 00:00:16.100
It's a scene that now looks prime

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:17.833
for the spread of the coronavirus.

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:21.230
- You have a lot of people together

00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:23.994
in one space for a long period of time

00:00:23.994 --> 00:00:25.560
without masks.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:27.810
We don't know if the
ventilation's any good,

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:29.982
and with activities like shouting,

00:00:29.982 --> 00:00:33.323
those are all the ingredients
for a super-spreading event.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:38.220
- [Narrator] That's why now,
school looks very different.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:42.020
Many schools are only partially
open for in-person learning,

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:44.310
and others are still fully remote.

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:45.808
- It's not business as usual,

00:00:45.808 --> 00:00:47.522
everything has changed.

00:00:47.522 --> 00:00:49.170
- [Narrator] Elia Valdes has been back

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:50.930
in her classroom since October.

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:53.980
- I was very concerned
about health issues.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:57.480
I knew that this was the
best thing for students,

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:00.940
to go in physically,
'cause that's hands-down,

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:02.374
they will always perform better.

00:01:02.374 --> 00:01:04.212
- [Narrator] With the
virus still spreading,

00:01:04.212 --> 00:01:06.640
can schools reopen safely?

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:09.230
What are the risks of
catching COVID there,

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:10.630
and how do control measures,

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:13.380
like wearing a mask or
increasing ventilation

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:14.970
change that picture?

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:17.720
Let's look at the classroom,
lunchroom, and break room.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:22.360
- Classrooms are one place where you have

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:25.150
a lot of people in the room
for a long period of time,

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:26.180
that's pretty risky.

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:27.550
- [Narrator] This is particularly true

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:29.710
for adults or older kids.

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:32.780
- Age really matters in
terms of transmission,

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:36.100
both in terms of acquisition of COVID,

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:38.630
as well as transmission of SARS-CoV-2,

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:40.210
the virus that causes COVID.

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:41.250
- [Narrator] Studies have found,

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:42.810
that compared with adults,

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:45.220
children might be infected less often,

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:47.780
are less likely to show severe symptoms,

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:49.542
and are less likely to be hospitalized

00:01:49.542 --> 00:01:51.756
for severe infection.

00:01:51.756 --> 00:01:56.560
That said, the risk isn't
zero, even for younger kids.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:58.313
We know that the virus is transmitted

00:01:58.313 --> 00:02:00.440
in a variety of ways.

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:03.180
Large droplets exchanged in a close range,

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:05.820
small droplets lingering in the air,

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:09.320
and indirectly through contact
with a contaminated surface.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:11.290
- If somebody who is infected with COVID

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:12.291
comes into a classroom

00:02:12.291 --> 00:02:14.920
and there's no control measures in place,

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:17.410
then they're sitting there
breathing and talking,

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:19.553
and they're releasing virus into the air.

00:02:19.553 --> 00:02:22.130
- [Narrator] Think of the
virus forming a small cloud

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:24.450
in front of the infected person.

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:26.250
Larger, virus-laden droplets

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:28.050
expelled from the nose or mouth,

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:30.450
would fall nearby, while
the smaller droplets

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:31.854
would float further away,

00:02:31.854 --> 00:02:34.540
and that's just from talking normally.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:36.964
- By shouting and screaming
and being emotional,

00:02:36.964 --> 00:02:39.680
you can give these droplets superpowers,

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:41.830
make them smaller, which we call aerosol,

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:46.410
and can fly further, 10,
20 feet in some cases.

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:48.600
- It's gonna kinda fill
the room eventually,

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:50.865
especially if that room
is poorly ventilated,

00:02:50.865 --> 00:02:52.830
and then everyone else in that room

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:53.663
is going to be exposed to it,

00:02:53.663 --> 00:02:55.773
and they'll be forced to breathe it.

00:02:56.903 --> 00:02:59.606
- [Narrator] But this
picture changes dramatically

00:02:59.606 --> 00:03:02.848
when control measures are put in place.

00:03:02.848 --> 00:03:06.840
Experts say the most
important one is masking.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.690
With masks on everyone,
the infected person

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:12.440
would release less virus into the air.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:14.480
And the others would have some protection

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:16.223
against the virus that remains.

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:19.080
- I would say the second
most important thing

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:22.241
is to ensure good
ventilation of the classroom.

00:03:22.241 --> 00:03:25.573
It could be as simple as
opening some doors and windows.

00:03:26.657 --> 00:03:28.510
- [Narrator] Doing so helps replace

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:29.930
the air inside the classroom,

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:33.458
bringing in clean air from outside.

00:03:33.458 --> 00:03:36.830
That can remove or reduce
the virus in the room,

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:38.563
lowering the risk of transmission.

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:43.000
Physical distancing is
another way to mitigate risk.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.740
The CDC used to recommend six feet,

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:47.910
but it's since changed it to three.

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:50.640
- There's not a magic number
for safe versus unsafe,

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:52.910
but the farther apart
people are, the better,

00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:54.160
so that they're kind of out

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:57.070
of each other's directly exhaled breath.

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:59.430
- [Narrator] A study of
Massachusetts Public Schools

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:00.520
found that case rates

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:03.140
in schools maintaining
three feet of distance

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:05.883
were similar to those of
schools using six feet.

00:04:07.811 --> 00:04:11.860
Prevention gets more
complicated in the lunchroom.

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:15.320
- So in the lunchroom
setting, it's virus heaven.

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:18.850
The virus is in paradise
because they have so many ways

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:21.174
to infect noses and mouths.

00:04:21.174 --> 00:04:22.820
- [Narrator] That's because to eat,

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:25.000
students need to take off their masks,

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:27.391
taking away a key control measure.

00:04:27.391 --> 00:04:29.639
To compensate, experts recommend

00:04:29.639 --> 00:04:31.830
adding portable air cleaners

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:34.240
with HEPA filters, if possible.

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:35.464
- The portable air cleaner works

00:04:35.464 --> 00:04:38.150
by pulling air through
it and passing it through

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:40.730
a really high quality filter

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:43.390
that filters out and
removes all the aerosols

00:04:43.390 --> 00:04:45.223
that are in the air and all the virus

00:04:45.223 --> 00:04:46.900
that they might contain.

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:49.370
And then out the other side
of the portable air cleaner

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:52.163
comes nice, clean, virus-free air.

00:04:53.151 --> 00:04:55.380
- [Narrator] In the teacher break room,

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:57.990
cleaning the air is also really important,

00:04:57.990 --> 00:04:59.490
because it's only adults.

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:02.280
- Adults, of course,
breathe more than kids do

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:04.410
and produce probably even per breath,

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:05.920
produce more aerosols.

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:07.640
And so let's say someone's sick,

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:10.100
well an adult is probably
gonna release more virus

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:12.230
into the air, and the
other adults in that room

00:05:12.230 --> 00:05:15.750
are gonna be breathing more
of that air compared to kids.

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:16.700
- [Narrator] Surface cleaning

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:19.540
and hand-washing are all important here,

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:22.610
just maybe not as critical
as was initially thought.

00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:24.560
- Excessive attention to surface cleaning

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:25.946
is taking away time and energy

00:05:25.946 --> 00:05:28.660
from cleaning the air, which
is gonna be more important

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:31.443
for reducing the risk of
transmission in schools.

00:05:33.022 --> 00:05:35.550
- [Narrator] For teachers, vaccination

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:37.866
is the latest control
measure, and most states

00:05:37.866 --> 00:05:40.730
are giving K through
12 educators priority.

00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:42.105
- I just recently got my vaccine,

00:05:42.105 --> 00:05:44.640
so that makes me feel a lot better.

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:45.787
- [Narrator] Even with a vaccine,

00:05:45.787 --> 00:05:49.370
limiting teacher interactions
with students is important.

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:50.203
- What can you do?

00:05:50.203 --> 00:05:51.570
- [Narrator] Because it's still unclear

00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:53.770
if vaccination stops transmission.

00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:56.180
And studies have shown
adults are more likely

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:58.360
to transmit the virus than younger kids,

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:00.750
for whom vaccines aren't authorized yet.

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:04.098
- I would always greet
my students at the door.

00:06:04.098 --> 00:06:06.352
Hello, how are you, come on in.

00:06:06.352 --> 00:06:08.470
Sit down, some of them would give me hugs.

00:06:08.470 --> 00:06:10.281
Oh, Miss Valdes, high five.

00:06:10.281 --> 00:06:15.012
Now it's like elbow or nope,
social distance, remember,

00:06:15.012 --> 00:06:17.674
we can't get close to one another.

00:06:17.674 --> 00:06:20.420
- [Narrator] Experts say
the key is not relying

00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:22.820
on any one control strategy.

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:25.848
Masking, distancing,
ventilation, vaccination,

00:06:25.848 --> 00:06:28.321
and testing all have a role to play

00:06:28.321 --> 00:06:30.629
in how schools reopen.

00:06:30.629 --> 00:06:33.790
- So to try and keep
everything all at once,

00:06:33.790 --> 00:06:35.780
and that laundry list in place,

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:39.045
it's probably going to be very
frustrating for many people.

00:06:39.045 --> 00:06:40.775
And I think if you had to do one thing,

00:06:40.775 --> 00:06:42.891
it's probably going to
be wearing that mask.

00:06:42.891 --> 00:06:45.724
(uplifting music)

